<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ivan Turgenev</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1818">1818</a>–<a href="/years/1883">1883</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ivan Sergeyevich Turgenev</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ivan Turgenev was a Russian novelist, short story writer, poet, playwright and translator, born in Oryol in 1818 to noble parents, who also made Russian literature known in the West. His collection A Sportsman's Sketches (1852) was a milestone of Russian realism and, like his story Mumu, took aim at the cruelties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a>. An obituary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikolai-gogol">Nikolai Gogol</a> in 1852 brought him a month in prison and exile to his country estate. He moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a> in 1854, living mostly at Baden-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> near the opera singer Pauline Viardot, and his novel Fathers and Sons (1862) is regarded as one of the major works of 19th-century fiction. He died at Bougival near Paris in 1883.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ivan-turgenev.jpg" alt="Ivan Turgenev" width="330" height="430" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ilya Repin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turgenev_by_Repin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Turgenev was born on 9 November 1818 (28 October in the Old Style calendar) in Oryol. His father, Sergei Nikolaevich Turgenev, was a cavalry colonel from an old but impoverished family; his mother, Varvara Petrovna Lutovinova, had inherited a huge fortune. Ivan and his brothers were raised by their authoritarian mother at the family estate of Spasskoye-Lutovinovo, with foreign governesses, and spoke French at home. She later served as an inspiration for the landlady in Mumu. He studied at the universities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> and from 1838 to 1841 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, where he studied philosophy, especially Hegel. He returned believing that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> could best improve itself by taking up the ideas of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>, and he was opposed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Turgenev made his name with A Sportsman's Sketches, stories drawn from peasant life and nature around his mother's estate, published in a single volume in 1852. The book is credited with influencing opinion in favour of the abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a> in 1861. In 1852 he wrote an obituary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikolai-gogol">Nikolai Gogol</a> that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> censor banned but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> censor allowed to appear; the censor was dismissed, and Turgenev was held responsible, imprisoned for a month and exiled to his estate for nearly two years. There he wrote Mumu in 1854, the tale of a deaf and mute peasant forced to drown his dog.</p><p>After he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a> in 1854, he produced his novels Rudin (1856), A Nest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">Gentry</a> (1859), On the Eve (1860) and Fathers and Sons (1862). Its leading character, Eugene Bazarov, was heralded and reviled as either a glorification or a parody of the new men of the 1860s, and the hostile reaction prompted him to leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and lose most of his readers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Turgenev's relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-tolstoy">Leo Tolstoy</a> and Fyodor Dostoyevsky were often strained: in 1861 Tolstoy challenged him to a duel and afterwards apologised, and the two did not speak for 17 years. In 1879 the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> conferred on him an honorary degree of Doctor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">Civil Law</a>. A malignant tumour was removed in January 1883, but it had spread, and on 3 September 1883 he died of a spinal abscess at Bougival. He was buried in the Volkovo Cemetery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>, and on his deathbed he urged Tolstoy: &quot;My friend, return to literature!&quot; Henry James and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-conrad">Joseph Conrad</a> preferred his artistic purity to Tolstoy and Dostoyevsky.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ivan Turgenev” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;In days of doubt, in days of dreary musings on my country's fate, thou alone art my stay and support, mighty, true, free Russian speech!&#8221;</p><footer>&quot;The Russian Tongue&quot;, Poems in Prose (1882) &#183; tr. Constance Garnett, 1897 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Dream_Tales_and_Prose_Poems/Poems_in_Prose/The_Russian_Tongue" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Love, I thought, is stronger than death or the fear of death. Only by it, by love, life holds together and advances.&#8221;</p><footer>&quot;The Sparrow&quot;, Poems in Prose (1882) &#183; tr. Constance Garnett, 1897 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Dream_Tales_and_Prose_Poems/Poems_in_Prose/The_Sparrow" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
